,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403317} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9f49c8-1095-5a46-8358-a01d4a4ae113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403318} {:answer/id #uuid "345e3ffa-dba9-5031-b6d1-880d3b6973b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403319} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9dd63b-07cb-5b7e-a9ff-84a037aad39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9dd63b-07cb-5b7e-a9ff-84a037aad39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403320} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c978983-f26a-5475-8ce3-d86bb0940128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403315}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99d45658-64c1-527b-ba91-3ee0b91560a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-6880" :question/problem "![](images/86/86dcfa13b7863c08b348855e776a6df709ecac499af02c2479cfdf484eeaf328.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d88f68b3-79d9-5a48-a7c2-f15131e65b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403983} {:answer/id #uuid "72609a03-e838-5ef4-baec-8bf52ebe1a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403984} {:answer/id #uuid "db076067-80ec-504a-b6e7-bc424311aecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403985} {:answer/id #uuid "1666ec87-7cf6-516a-a70f-0da860b50921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403986} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb36cf6-f9c5-5daa-a5a9-b549f1488f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1666ec87-7cf6-516a-a70f-0da860b50921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403986} :answer-field/id #uuid "72801a88-c8c5-56f3-a725-309aa394dd69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403982}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9798ed5-9467-5b84-a966-140686dec581" :question/math-academy-id "q-79281" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac2bba143473bc68e3a578c008a5de3cc36727d984bfba5d5e0da95462c08f77.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152188 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151990 :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7d0b49-a86d-5dac-9980-5a31f3551190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333419} {:answer/id #uuid "8094e70b-c370-5011-a1fa-b202845cf12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334132} {:answer/id #uuid "a772a1dd-6c85-59e3-9ae1-db65d223e432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334456} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4207fe-16d7-587f-9134-5cb8b7f5cc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334518} {:answer/id #uuid "d56b2bc6-e730-5565-a0d6-c383d13ce2a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a772a1dd-6c85-59e3-9ae1-db65d223e432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334456} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6e66ab-9753-5c19-896c-fc7be08555c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335258}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be3693e3-b291-55cc-9e6f-d294ae6aac43" :question/math-academy-id "q-6881" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3754726d-eb07-5177-bde1-2e130a929394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345826} {:answer/id #uuid "41933836-9e91-5567-ae93-cdca54f3119c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+(y+1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345910} {:answer/id #uuid "634f6c56-30af-534a-9acf-909105673bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346215} {:answer/id #uuid "85b84dc3-77f6-5317-bc63-cba7718f74d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+(y+1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346513} {:answer/id #uuid "97060e97-47b0-5626-85ea-a37318a871be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97060e97-47b0-5626-85ea-a37318a871be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346673} :answer-field/id #uuid "16df9bb4-f58f-542f-8d1a-7a5f96d7b152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347649}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddcf2b55-d913-5f98-8f2e-d38863acbf2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79375" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2739d8-8263-5715-80a0-b3b7c6968f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238306} {:answer/id #uuid "382f295d-a445-5d8b-bc0f-6ace3caf95de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238540} {:answer/id #uuid "73aae932-0595-5347-8815-ae9f181c5b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239045} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2d8986-d88d-5b5c-9f11-422be6546780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239162} {:answer/id #uuid "c61dff78-fa4e-581e-a2bf-f0e93446cb93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73aae932-0595-5347-8815-ae9f181c5b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239045} :answer-field/id #uuid "898c248a-58ee-5aa9-8564-479985c71b0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240576}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8cd9927-85e7-5cc4-8971-2d06dd8aa5a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106405" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430731d8-2746-561b-9932-67b216b9fe6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238637} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5367ef-504b-5ba3-a55b-c4c01e11de99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238996} {:answer/id #uuid "e04c2016-5bb4-58d5-b9d6-a2cd96a09d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240017} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b0efb3-ded6-535b-9129-9f1e0979660f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240025} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f34c9d-d585-551d-ad3a-8864f684d38c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "430731d8-2746-561b-9932-67b216b9fe6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238637} :answer-field/id #uuid "e50ff31d-bfd9-5fd4-b0bd-dcf00ea22347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240730}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6677d4ff-0012-5e92-83bc-c53813937d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106406" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c85e08-cf6a-5307-80f4-ce01ca0ebcac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238422} {:answer/id #uuid "45c84b8d-f295-5003-a523-bb16c6bcf8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238658} {:answer/id #uuid "689800b2-34c6-5ad7-a7b0-9045f353fb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238964} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4de9d6-48b4-5098-a575-119212df9e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239570} {:answer/id #uuid "c35b39d0-2b50-56f0-b092-5a399f164c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35b39d0-2b50-56f0-b092-5a399f164c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239778} :answer-field/id #uuid "45dde714-85df-57b8-888c-f62cc7cc39a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240450}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b47425b-d1be-54fd-a4d5-ddff6578df69" :question/math-academy-id "q-106407" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0363f299-ebce-5cd3-a3d9-6edbee6ef1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{(x-2)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238110} {:answer/id #uuid "09798478-efa7-5adb-b8d0-a50d61788683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x-2)}^{2}-4{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238149} {:answer/id #uuid "117cba79-f965-5374-92ac-bc6e684c94b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x-3)}^{2}+4{(y-2)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238201} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1fc9c6-aa5a-5af7-8fe4-228cbf131588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239567} {:answer/id #uuid "d42beaa6-50c5-5dd1-9084-6b775db8f4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d42beaa6-50c5-5dd1-9084-6b775db8f4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239919} :answer-field/id #uuid "57eda148-ff6d-5235-8674-30b291d678a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240493}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6811d7d-6469-50eb-bfd3-5c7960859395" :question/math-academy-id "q-106408" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365c4773-e368-5e9e-b8d3-09d97f8cf201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241658} {:answer/id #uuid "6115395b-4dd0-5476-84c1-62a064355b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242054} {:answer/id #uuid "718e50be-5a17-5e14-88c8-db7be4da154a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242189} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ffe555-2803-571a-a13e-b08b1c573270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242663} {:answer/id #uuid "db656494-d26d-54fd-a2be-e62b928fc054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6115395b-4dd0-5476-84c1-62a064355b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242054} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7931ea-4640-57b6-8a13-7f713b36ad36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243514}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00eda352-d2e8-5490-8b6e-f1ece02f57de" :question/math-academy-id "q-106715" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8f1b2b-173b-5b92-b859-1ec4e15fc869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272505} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0ec060-43c6-5507-a4b4-71bf27bfdb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272798} {:answer/id #uuid "3d24278c-1604-5a0e-92c8-91b968fed491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272912} {:answer/id #uuid "6793780e-4123-58f7-8dac-1bc0e63caa25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273279} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9ffd0c-5022-5c83-b63e-3c3cbf9ed38c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0ec060-43c6-5507-a4b4-71bf27bfdb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272798} :answer-field/id #uuid "30cc30d9-eb2f-5ad6-9d67-21da1b5948c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274721}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ccbcc93-cb2d-5c7d-ae5c-a7e728fdad2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15792" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044aa764-a402-587b-9d4c-b41637dfa030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275516} {:answer/id #uuid "8324a325-41e1-54bc-8b74-1c52b4799e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276657} {:answer/id #uuid "c4127d31-6dc3-521f-8215-809b40a67775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277220} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c6cd3a-dd09-5f85-a646-0e12c483a833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277337} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c3aad5-29e3-5b96-b737-92612b8b8109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2c3aad5-29e3-5b96-b737-92612b8b8109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277461} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b8f849-09df-52e8-a517-f7b406de368f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48febb37-71be-5629-b005-ff375ac1902f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15843" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2506dc2c-9a48-5ddf-a190-045af9bb3db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2506dc2c-9a48-5ddf-a190-045af9bb3db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300138} :answer-field/id #uuid "e51629cd-762c-5f50-ba6f-6feeb02fa23d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301952}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcd20c00-207d-5776-ad99-e9ea0fea55dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-327798" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{y+5}{2\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc28628-0500-5a4e-b7c4-aab08e1def7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bc28628-0500-5a4e-b7c4-aab08e1def7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300578} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b576c6-b6ea-547c-b701-c6162e1d6269" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301296}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf7a561b-81b3-5465-9ed7-22d55b60ba48" :question/math-academy-id "q-327803" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter  $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{10} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12 \\\\ \\frac{y-12}{3\\sqrt{11}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ec0879-40ef-5eca-bcab-024dcd2d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78ec0879-40ef-5eca-bcab-024dcd2d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300557} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7318c6-8514-5dcc-b694-eb9615ec63ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301737}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42002e7f-0b06-57fa-9bb9-6d321bc37cf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-327808" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab159e0e-467d-5f21-8fde-88f54619f27f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab159e0e-467d-5f21-8fde-88f54619f27f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300821} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d87d77-faf2-51e3-8007-0a1b765c8eff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301356}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c43bad9-0cfd-52b5-8797-68675d788468" :question/math-academy-id "q-327809" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{48} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300280} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdeb47f-480e-5ca4-a46f-7f342cc63ce7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301369}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4592b8-c042-55bc-b24f-4355e15f5f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327811" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad1b940-29ef-5bb3-ab76-b9022d36ef57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345436} {:answer/id #uuid "5bab3d83-01ac-56d9-87c7-89af73d69a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346134} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ebee1-e81a-5755-a697-cbac27922e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346426} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3731bc-0af6-503e-8640-17df73965f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346446} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7fa320-1feb-5ea9-b5c0-8430abb685cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3731bc-0af6-503e-8640-17df73965f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346446} :answer-field/id #uuid "21a04564-f6ec-5bf2-ab6f-b6795ab6636b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347662}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9015a8d2-7682-57fa-9d91-2827376ba1a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79377" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{x+5}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2 \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151991 :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](images/47/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ac84a3-564f-5087-9c72-21270fad982d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\cos θ - 2, y = 2\\sin θ - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151278} {:answer/id #uuid "15b4fd37-f6f5-5cb5-8179-675f402f3250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 8\\cos θ + 2, y = 4\\sin θ + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151279} {:answer/id #uuid "1dce4f69-f8fb-56de-8e99-fa6fab6ae60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2\\cos θ + 2, y = 1\\sin θ - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151280} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee1a14-7fba-532f-9dfa-2441efbaafe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\cos θ + 2, y = 2\\sin θ + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151281} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2ff814-2525-5b63-ab38-11ed1b67b077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 8\\cos θ - 2, y = 4\\sin θ - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151282}] :answer-fie