$\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c83eb61b-d093-55e1-8524-2613dce4c86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c83eb61b-d093-55e1-8524-2613dce4c86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291491} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3def395-9142-57f7-80c2-7ffaf2820a72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0540bab6-0f5a-5dbc-8773-de1617adc993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0540bab6-0f5a-5dbc-8773-de1617adc993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290661} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcee20d9-5ee6-569d-8ebf-8c9c64747a52" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292518}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f43b2664-c814-5c17-82ea-86e156e4166f" :question/math-academy-id "q-274866" :question/problem "![](images/06/061c7c34639d88b72d1a3045e794e2aa149b3331dafa33447071aed80b861088.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/6d/6dc795d39f90b6df177fe815a9135e7eadbe0b0d7d01214009de495e704486f7.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b63df4a4-ed4e-594d-a87c-7a4085320bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b63df4a4-ed4e-594d-a87c-7a4085320bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300869} :answer-field/id #uuid "00924e31-27a5-58f1-944d-ca579e1c3713" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301233} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74de50fd-2ab8-57fe-9cfb-ece3bde403ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74de50fd-2ab8-57fe-9cfb-ece3bde403ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/