le formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/24/24b56c8addea0d8a0f3effeeefc2788023c452e98b05d10db7893aeebde9ba5e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa59ec8d-cc80-5c21-9a98-1a321275e779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa59ec8d-cc80-5c21-9a98-1a321275e779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301198} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b1ece4-081f-5e29-9bb2-7e66dd6a50dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3ca0ddb-ad18-53c8-8fa4-f75ebc48934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ca0ddb-ad18-53c8-8fa4-f75ebc48934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300788} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ef5771-df19-54b8-9c2c-99c1a01e2acc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301956}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50c33ba2-db10-58a1-9a90-99dd50ad5588" :question/math-academy-id "q-327831" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5bf161de62bc1b38aaeb87a327df551e8a7043f2867caa060d652cd065bece9.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6efc63426eae95430b0b5ddee44ab1523f69eb00ba26720374f7246712f7088a.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse in the **fourth quadrant** (where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0$).\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\np=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171799d9-7223-5dd4-a228-48e8aba86de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "171799d9-7223-5dd4-a228-48e8aba86de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300055} :answer-field/id #uuid "75df719e-462a-58a0-914e-a2e113fe9c32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301591} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fff4831-cbfc-507b-91dd-e522a76e056b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300378}] :