* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we again interpret $\\theta $ as the central angle measured from the horizontal line through the center $(h,k).$ On the quarter-arc where both $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ (moving from the rightmost point to the topmost point), the parameter must satisfy $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n![](images/df/df6c12657a6a487f93ec3a6213be8ea16e68d363001bdf0ddb8ff68b5201a8c4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eecdf539-f4c9-5e2a-b1d3-147515693798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eecdf539-f4c9-5e2a-b1d3-147515693798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301145} :answer-field/id #uuid "908f284e-1908-5bfa-9308-7074a7ede550" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301687} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4981ef-e57a-51b9-b310-d03c74ee1689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4981ef-e57a-51b9-b310-d03c74ee1689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301053} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34a02c9-d0c9-59ab-b604-48db38e67691" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301945}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e29b9a7-ba3d-59be-8f85-186fc6a70498" :question/math-academy-id "q-328327" :question/problem "![](images/52/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](images/1b/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9f41de-144e-5379-8d7e-1e17fed1cc44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345584} {:answer/id #uuid "60811d34-2531-5a15-97a2-b323f7a440ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346186} {:answer/id #uuid "636af2c9-f212-547b-ae0a-6bee08eb660c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346216} {:answer/id #uuid "c53b6a3c-e3e4-57a8-b745-a6cea4f29a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347059} {:answer/id #uuid "c883c73e-dd77-569e-a6ca-c43b10cb3266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60811d34-2531-5a15-97a2-b323f7a440ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346186} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb353e1-f2f0-5b09-9159-dfbd37e1459f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347798}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c0805a2-0226-5053-b156-be4380c657d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-79273" :question/problem "![](images/48/48c5190fc4f45f4bf3e76a56977339559ceb30ca90a41411d3bc4df2362b0787.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/54/546b9f72124a7eb05877db3600e40b5772404a1a3378de67cfbe3320c78ac2ba.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a18201-8079-51dd-b682-32f608606117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345389} {:answer/id #uuid "2db8c32c-4882-57c9-b92f-dcdfe7b0c6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757} {:answer/id #uuid "56f5788a-a5a6-5e32-946c-bf55db053ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346091} {:answer/id #uuid "8855c7fb-4098-5cd5-b05a-21eccba165f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346533} {:answer/id #uuid "b8010337-6cef-584f-b257-a06f6c10ce39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db8c32c-4882-57c9-b92f-dcdfe7b0c6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea18173-d655-51a6-a7a1-312a50186ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347959}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de3651a5-52a0-5b3e-bfe6-600c2b9ae816" :question/math-academy-id "q-79275" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe1a9cf99e5a3b2cae80a4bfd01cdcabdc77560ccbb17ff9c7aa580a532d114.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5d/5d615d9fd29a09345b05ee4d80ffdc49198924d0cd44edb9c815270543fae60f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200dc369-b95f-5003-9958-273f823a2f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345635} {:answer/id #uuid "6a200e6f-b5ac-52f6-972d-b2490377dfd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346271} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5c6e0b-35eb-59a1-8aa0-be651e5193c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346461} {:answer/id #uuid "baa5e144-ff07-5209-b88f-ab79ce37dd1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346972} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0019f-f524-5a56-94b3-fd7d7d44bbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0019f-f524-5a56-94b3-fd7d7d44bbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347573} :answer-field/id #uuid "90acc913-e06e-54ed-a8a3-00b923284f02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e37c1011-13e6-56e9-875d-43a2f977ac1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79280" :question/problem "![](images/75/7540559d86e836d869173da11ca0f7befba8ead07c844be1c9708591fb372042.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/30/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ae9e4d5-1932-5800-b236-37087405939a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345992} {:answer/id #uuid "505ca61e-01ab-50bc-97db-89bb72239096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346038} {:answer/id #uuid "6954d44a-60b3-5e34-b236-c0ac6a4e9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346264} {:answer/id #uuid "872a8b7e-64bf-58c4-849a-c3251db6aa47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346524} {:answer/id #uuid "e79a502f-c6aa-5e97-9b7a-0b468f676d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6954d44a-60b3-5e34-b236-c0ac6a4e9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346264} :answer-field/id #uuid "bae4996f-a9a8-5af8-9940-afb0ac743da4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347933}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49ccb09f-0fa1-51f9-9419-f7f713cf6b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-79282" :question/problem "![](images/16/1617a8efd9110fa435dc396d90450c7fe17d18105e2358ab5610135e32a385ad.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,-1),$ $a=4$ and $b=2.$  Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/46/4659fda20ed9a2b5e5c10d60f504e4db378efb0bfe9ae037f4998511e75300cb.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}]}
